<<<
envelop. See -p-, -PP-. The obsolete spellings envelope, envelopes, in the verb, are now to be regarded as mere mistakes.
===
**envelop.** See ^^-P-^^, ^^-PP-^^. The obsolete spellings *envelope, envelopes,* in the verb, are now to be regarded as mere mistakes.
>>>

<<<
envelope. The French spelling (-ppe) has long gone, & the French pronunciation should no longer be allowed to embarrass us, but give way to é’nvelop; all the more now that the verb envelop, from its frequency in military bulletins, has become popular instead of merely literary.
===
**envelope.** The French spelling *(-ppe)* has long gone, & the French pronunciation should no longer be allowed to embarrass us, but give way to ĕ'nvelōp; all the more now that the verb *envelop*, from its frequency in military bulletins, has become popular instead of merely literary.
>>>

<<<
-EN VERBS FROM ADJECTIVES. It being no part of most people’s business to inquire into such matters, the average writer would probably say, if asked for an offhand opinion, that from any adjective of one syllable an -en verb could be formed
===
**-EN VERBS FROM ADJECTIVES.** It being no part of most people's business to inquire into such matters, the average writer would probably say, if asked for an offhand opinion, that from any adjective of one syllable an *-en* verb could be formed
>>>

<<<
ago on the merits of quieten. A very slight examination shows it to be remote from the facts; -en cannot be called a living suffix.
===
ago on the merits of *quieten*. A very slight examination shows it to be remote from the facts; *-en* cannot be called a living suffix.
>>>

<<<
by addition of -en as the 50, no-one would dream of treating in that way; some of them are allowed to become verbs without the -en (lame, wet, blind, foul); others have to go without a cognate verb (harsh, grand, wise, sore);
===
by addition of *-en* as the 50, no-one would dream of treating in that way; some of them are allowed to become verbs without the *-en* (*lame, wet, blind, foul*); others have to go without a cognate verb (*harsh, grand, wise, sore*);
>>>

<<<
others have their beginning operated on instead of their end (large & enlarge, fine & refine, new & renew, plain & explain, strange & estrange, dense & condense);
===
others have their beginning operated on instead of their end (*large & enlarge, fine & refine, new & renew, plain & explain, strange & estrange, dense & condense*);
>>>

<<<
& the despotism of usage is still clearer when it is noticed that we can say moisten but not wetten, quicken but not slowen, thicken & fatten but not thinnen or leanen,
===
& the despotism of usage is still clearer when it is noticed that we can say *moisten* but not *wetten*, *quicken* but not *slowen*, *thicken* & *fatten* but not *thinnen* or *leanen*,
>>>

<<<
deafen but not blinden, sweeten but not souren, sharpen but not blunten, cheapen but not dearen, greaten but not largen, freshen but not stalen, coarsen but not finen.
===
*deafen* but not *blinden*, *sweeten* but not *souren*, *sharpen* but not *blunten*, *cheapen* but not *dearen*, *greaten* but not *largen*, *freshen* but not *stalen*, *coarsen* but not *finen*.
>>>

<<<
some taking & some refusing -en, there are a few about
===
some taking & some refusing *-en*, there are a few about
>>>

<<<
with some the right of the -en verb to exist is disputable, & with others the undoubted existence of two verbs (e.g. loose & loosen), one having -en, & one identical with the adjective, raises the question of differentiated senses;
===
with some the right of the *-en* verb to exist is disputable, & with others the undoubted existence of two verbs (e.g. *loose & loosen*), one having *-en*, & one identical with the adjective, raises the question of differentiated senses;
>>>

<<<
of the ordinary -en verbs, not including anomalous ones like strengthen, nor any whose right to exist is dubious:— blacken, brighten, broaden, cheapen,
===
of the ordinary *-en* verbs, not including anomalous ones like *strengthen*, nor any whose right to exist is dubious:—*blacken, brighten, broaden, cheapen,*
>>>

